# Enrich

A unit's dependencies and **grants are its imports** — the capabilities the [[The
Dock]] must fulfill before it runs. You never write WIT; you declare what you need.

`grant net: "api.crm.com"` imports one capability: network calls, **only** to that
host. Reaching `evil.example` would be refused at the seam. Grants flow by explicit
grant, never inheritance — a unit that calls `:enrich` doesn't inherit its net.

server :enrich, grant: [net: "api.crm.com"] do
  # A with-railway: each `<-` must match `{:ok, _}` to proceed; any other shape drops
  # to `else`. The happy path reads top-to-bottom, the two failure shapes are handled
  # once. `Dock.fetch` is the brokered call — the membrane checks our grant before the
  # request leaves the nexus and hands back a variant, never an exception.
  def enrich(%Lead{name: name} = lead) do
    with {:ok, %{status: 200, body: body}} <- Dock.fetch(crm_url(name)),
         {:ok, account}                    <- parse(body) do
      %Lead{lead | domain: account.domain, revenue: account.revenue,
                   employees: account.employees, status: :enriched}
    else
      {:ok, %{status: 404}} -> lead          # not in the CRM yet; leave as-is
      {:error, _reason}     -> lead          # transient; a later pass retries
    end
  end

  defp crm_url(name), do: "https://api.crm.com/v1/accounts?name=" <> URI.encode(name)
end

Fallibility is modelled as a **tagged tuple** — `{:ok, _} | {:error, reason}` — so
the value's shape carries success or failure. Because `:enrich` is pure apart from
one brokered call, the [[Pipeline]] can run it in parallel and the gate can audit
that single grant in isolation. Next: [[Pipeline]].
